		<item>
		<title>PICC P&#038;C’s new $12m Great Wall Re is the first Hong Kong sidecar ILS issuance</title>
		<link>https://www.artemis.bm/news/picc-pcs-new-12m-great-wall-re-is-the-first-hong-kong-sidecar-ils-issuance/?utm_source=rss&#038;utm_medium=rss&#038;utm_campaign=picc-pcs-new-12m-great-wall-re-is-the-first-hong-kong-sidecar-ils-issuance</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Steve Evans]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 10:08:41 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Reinsurance News]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Great Wall Re Limited]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[hong kong]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Insurance linked securities]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[reinsurance]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Reinsurance linked investment]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[sidecar]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.artemis.bm/?p=150980</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>This content is copyright to <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.artemis.bm</a> and should not appear anywhere else, or an infringement has occurred.</p>
<p>PICC Property and Casualty Company Limited has now reported that its new US $12 million issuance of insurance-linked securities under its Great Wall Re Limited special purpose insurer is actually a quota share reinsurance sidecar, the first in Hong Kong, designed to provide protection to its offshore wind power property insurance book on the Chinese [&#8230;]</p>
<p><a href="https://www.artemis.bm/news/picc-pcs-new-12m-great-wall-re-is-the-first-hong-kong-sidecar-ils-issuance/">PICC P&#038;C’s new $12m Great Wall Re is the first Hong Kong sidecar ILS issuance</a> was published by: <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.Artemis.bm</a><br />
Our <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/deal-directory/">catastrophe bond deal directory</a><br />
Sign up for <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/e-newsletter/">our free weekly email newsletter here</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>This content is copyright to <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.artemis.bm</a> and should not appear anywhere else, or an infringement has occurred.</p>
<p>PICC Property and Casualty Company Limited has now reported that its new US $12 million issuance of insurance-linked securities under its Great Wall Re Limited special purpose insurer is actually a quota share reinsurance sidecar, the first in Hong Kong, designed to provide protection to its offshore wind power property insurance book on the Chinese Mainland.<br />
<span id="more-150980"></span><br />
<img decoding="async" data-attachment-id="97765" data-permalink="https://www.artemis.bm/news/great-wall-re-catastrophe-bond-issued-in-hong-kong-for-picc-pc/attachment/picc-pc-insurance-logo/" data-orig-file="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/picc-pc-insurance-logo.jpg" data-orig-size="776,477" data-comments-opened="0" data-image-title="picc-pc-insurance-logo" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-large-file="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/picc-pc-insurance-logo.jpg" class="alignright wp-image-97765" src="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/picc-pc-insurance-logo.jpg" alt="picc-pc-insurance-logo" width="360" height="221" srcset="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/picc-pc-insurance-logo.jpg 776w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/picc-pc-insurance-logo-300x184.jpg 300w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/picc-pc-insurance-logo-768x472.jpg 768w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/picc-pc-insurance-logo-200x123.jpg 200w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/picc-pc-insurance-logo-350x215.jpg 350w" sizes="(max-width: 360px) 100vw, 360px" />Given the limited information, we were incorrect in initially reporting that the new Great Wall Re issuance we had learned about was a catastrophe bond, having made the assumption it was a follow-up to PICC Property and Casualty Company&#8217;s debut sponsorship back in late 2022, the $32.5 million <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/deal-directory/great-wall-re-limited/">Great Wall Re Limited</a> cat bond deal.</p>
<p>PICC Property and Casualty Company has instead utilised Hong Kong&#8217;s insurance-linked securities regulatory regime to become the first collateralised reinsurance sidecar sponsor to use an SPI located there.</p>
<p>We had <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/news/picc-pc-appears-to-have-returned-for-its-second-great-wall-re-catastrophe-bond/">reported on October 5th</a> that a new Great Wall Re issuance of insurance-linked securities had been completed, assuming it to be another cat bond from the sponsor, then following up a few days later after <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/news/picc-pc-new-great-wall-re-catastrophe-bond-said-a-12m-issuance/">we learned it was a US $12 million (HK $94m) issuance of notes</a>.</p>
<p>Now, it&#8217;s clear that this is a landmark first quota share reinsurance sidecar securitisation for Hong Kong, as the country successfully expands the use-cases for its ILS infrastructure.</p>
<p>This morning PICC Property and Casualty Company has said that the US $12 million (HK $94m) Great Wall Re ILS issuance is &#8220;the first issuance of insurance-linked securities with a &#8220;sidecar&#8221; structure in the Hong Kong market,&#8221; and the first issuance of sidecar insurance-linked securities by any domestic Chinese insurance company.</p>
<p>It&#8217;s a relatively unique deal as well given it provides reinsurance to both PICC People&#8217;s Insurance and Property Insurance covering offshore wind power property risks in mainland China, certainly an infrequently seen risk profile for the ILS market, perhaps the first time the market has seen these risks in ILS form.</p>
<p>The insurer said this is &#8220;another innovative practice for the company to use the international capital market to spread insurance risks after the successful issuance of disaster bonds in Hong Kong in 2022.&#8221;</p>
<p>Being a sidecar, the underlying subject reinsurance arrangement with this US $12 million Great Wall Re deal is a quota share, or proportional reinsurance, transferring the risks to capital market investors on that basis.</p>
<p>The Hong Kong based SPI raises capital to fund the risks by issuing equity or debt securities, with those funds deposited in trust accounts and invested in low-risk, highly liquid assets, paying the investors backed the deal over-time. The income and loss of the insurance-linked securities are linked to the profit and loss of the sidecar&#8217;s proportional reinsurance contracts.</p>
<p>PICC P&amp;C explained, &#8220;Its issuance structure reflects the core characteristics of income sharing, risk sharing and flexible loading and unloading between insurance companies and capital market investors, so it is called &#8220;sidecar&#8221;. The &#8220;sidecar&#8221; insurance-linked securities and disaster bonds that spread risks through super-compensation reinsurance complement each other, which provides new tools for Chinese insurance companies to expand risk diversification channels and enhance underwriting capabilities.&#8221;</p>
<p>PICC P&amp;C further stated, &#8220;This issue introduces the risk of China&#8217;s offshore wind power insurance into the international capital market, enhances international investors&#8217; understanding of China&#8217;s green energy insurance business, and expands the risk diversification channel for the development of green energy for insurance services.</p>
<p>&#8220;The insurance-linked securities previously issued in the Hong Kong market are all disaster bond structures. This &#8220;sidecar&#8221; issuance enriches the transaction structure and business scenario of the Hong Kong insurance-linked securities market, which helps to promote the coordinated development of the Hong Kong insurance market, capital market and professional service institutions, and helps improve the Hong Kong International Financial Center. Function, improve the global competitiveness of Hong Kong International Financial Center, and inject new momentum into the financial innovation of the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area.</p>
<p>&#8220;In the next step, People&#8217;s Insurance and Property Insurance will continue to practice political and people&#8217;s requirements, coordinate development and security, deepen the exploration of insurance risk securitization, continuously broaden the risk dispersion channels, improve the risk protection capacity of large disasters, and contribute to the construction of the national disaster risk protection system and the construction of the Hong Kong International Financial Center.&#8221;</p>
<p>Hong Kong&#8217;s Financial Services &amp; Treasury Bureau said that it &#8220;welcomed the inaugural issuance of insurance-linked securities (ILS) in the form of a “sidecar” by the PICC Property &amp; Casualty Company (PICC P&amp;C) in Hong Kong.&#8221;</p>
<p>Secretary for Financial Services &amp; the Treasury Christopher Hui commented, “We welcome PICC P&amp;C’s issuance of Hong Kong’s first ‘sidecar’. This demonstrates Hong Kong’s combined strengths in reinsurance and capital markets.</p>
<p>“The issuance marks significant progress gained for Hong Kong’s insurance industry and capital markets in risk transfer and the use of alternative capital under the support of our country. It will further enhance the market’s capacity to manage catastrophe and other specialty risks, and consolidate Hong Kong’s position as an international risk management centre.</p>
<p>“This will enable us to unleash the synergy effect of multilayered risk transfer and pooling mechanisms through ILS and other tools, thus consolidating our strategic role as a regional and global catastrophe and specialty risk management hub.</p>
<p>“ILS and captive insurance, which we have long been promoting, can provide enterprises ‘going global’ with diversified means of spreading and managing risks.</p>
<p>“We expect this combination of financial policy measures will allow enterprises to leverage Hong Kong as a safe haven for &#8216;going global&#8217; with greater ease of mind.”</p>
<p class="text_50">Clement Cheung, Chief Executive Officer of the Insurance Authority added, “Riding on policy support from the National Financial Regulatory Administration, this ILS issuance fully demonstrates the commitment and determination with which we pursue the development of Hong Kong into a global risk management centre.</p>
<p class="text_50">“The IA will also sustain efforts to facilitate introduction of protected cell company as an ILS structure and review the existing definition of eligible ILS investors for a more vibrant trading market.&#8221;</p>
<p>Now that we understand this new Great Wall Re insurance-linked securities arrangement for PICC P&amp;C is a quota share reinsurance sidecar deal we have removed it from our catastrophe bond Deal Directory and added it to <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/library/reinsurance_sidecars.html">our directory of reinsurance sidecar transactions</a>.</p>
<p>It&#8217;s encouraging to see the expansion of Hong Kong&#8217;s ILS regime to incorporate sidecar arrangements and also good to see the unique use-case to cover offshore wind power risks in China.</p>
<p>Find details of numerous reinsurance sidecar investments and transactions, including life reinsurance sidecar structures, in our directory of <strong><a href="https://www.artemis.bm/library/reinsurance_sidecars.html">collateralized reinsurance sidecar transactions</a></strong>.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.artemis.bm/news/picc-pcs-new-12m-great-wall-re-is-the-first-hong-kong-sidecar-ils-issuance/">PICC P&#038;C’s new $12m Great Wall Re is the first Hong Kong sidecar ILS issuance</a> was published by: <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.Artemis.bm</a><br />
Our <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/deal-directory/">catastrophe bond deal directory</a><br />
Sign up for <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/e-newsletter/">our free weekly email newsletter here</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
		<post-id xmlns="com-wordpress:feed-additions:1">150980</post-id>	</item>
		<item>
		<title>In casualty ILS motivations are key, timelines must be realistic: Trahan, Calidris</title>
		<link>https://www.artemis.bm/news/in-casualty-ils-motivations-are-key-timelines-must-be-realistic-trahan-calidris/?utm_source=rss&#038;utm_medium=rss&#038;utm_campaign=in-casualty-ils-motivations-are-key-timelines-must-be-realistic-trahan-calidris</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Steve Evans]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 08:00:41 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[Insurance Linked Investments]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Calidris Investment Partners]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Casualty ILS]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Casualty insurance-linked securities]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[casualty sidecar]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Insurance linked securities]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Insurance-linked investments]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Reinsurance linked investment]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[sidecar]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.artemis.bm/?p=150971</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>This content is copyright to <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.artemis.bm</a> and should not appear anywhere else, or an infringement has occurred.</p>
<p>Casualty insurance-linked securities transactions are complex and require strong alignment, which makes finding strategic partners and understanding their motivations key, while at the same time given the complexity of arrangements such as casualty sidecars, realistic timelines are important, Philippe Trahan of Calidris Investment Partners said yesterday. Trahan, Co-founder and Co-CEO of insurance-focused investment manager Calidris [&#8230;]</p>
<p><a href="https://www.artemis.bm/news/in-casualty-ils-motivations-are-key-timelines-must-be-realistic-trahan-calidris/">In casualty ILS motivations are key, timelines must be realistic: Trahan, Calidris</a> was published by: <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.Artemis.bm</a><br />
Our <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/deal-directory/">catastrophe bond deal directory</a><br />
Sign up for <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/e-newsletter/">our free weekly email newsletter here</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>This content is copyright to <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.artemis.bm</a> and should not appear anywhere else, or an infringement has occurred.</p>
<p>Casualty insurance-linked securities transactions are complex and require strong alignment, which makes finding strategic partners and understanding their motivations key, while at the same time given the complexity of arrangements such as casualty sidecars, realistic timelines are important, Philippe Trahan of Calidris Investment Partners said yesterday.<br />
<span id="more-150971"></span><br />
<img decoding="async" data-attachment-id="129445" data-permalink="https://www.artemis.bm/news/calidris-investment-partners-launched-by-philippe-trahan-and-daniel-miller/attachment/calidris-investment-partners-logo/" data-orig-file="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/calidris-investment-partners-logo.png" data-orig-size="800,500" data-comments-opened="0" data-image-title="calidris-investment-partners-logo" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-large-file="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/calidris-investment-partners-logo.png" class="alignright wp-image-129445" src="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/calidris-investment-partners-logo.png" alt="calidris-investment-partners-logo" width="360" height="225" srcset="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/calidris-investment-partners-logo.png 800w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/calidris-investment-partners-logo-300x188.png 300w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/calidris-investment-partners-logo-768x480.png 768w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/calidris-investment-partners-logo-200x125.png 200w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/calidris-investment-partners-logo-350x219.png 350w" sizes="(max-width: 360px) 100vw, 360px" />Trahan, Co-founder and Co-CEO of insurance-focused investment manager Calidris Investment Partners, was speaking during a casualty ILS focused panel session at the ILS Bermuda Convergence 2026 conference yesterday.</p>
<p>As a company with a focus on opportunities in these longer-tailed areas of insurance and reinsurance, Trahan noted that Calidris recognises the time required to both get comfortable with counterparties and asset managers involved in casualty transactions, as well as being cognisant of the time needed to get to well-structured and aligned deal outcomes.</p>
<p>&#8220;What we spend a lot of time on, we&#8217;re really more focused on finding partners, establishing strategic partnerships. What we want to avoid is we don&#8217;t want to be a hedge. So therefore motivations as to why, whether it&#8217;s an MGA or broker facility these days, an insurer or reinsurer that is considering wanting to launch a sidecar, why is it that they&#8217;re doing it, and what are their motivations?,&#8221; Trahan explained.</p>
<p>&#8220;What I find is then, it&#8217;s a bit of a different approach. I would say it&#8217;s a market that was mostly done on a bilateral basis between investors and counterparties, and so this year with the kind of syndicated market, which is a normal and natural evolution, it actually shows that it&#8217;s growing, it has legs.</p>
<p>&#8220;It does bring some additional complexity, and there&#8217;s a realisation that&#8217;s required on the broker or counterparty side. Once you&#8217;re comfortable that their motivations are trying to strive to achieve a partnership, that will ideally be scalable and repeatable, these require a lot of time from the management suite. So they don&#8217;t do this for a one-year trade and just disappear. So that&#8217;s quite helpful.&#8221;</p>
<p>He went on to suggest that timelines need to be realistic, as the complexity and work-effort involved in casualty ILS deals means that they are unlikely to be completed particularly quickly.</p>
<p>Trahan said, &#8220;The other obstacle is, we find, unrealistic timelines. These sidecars take six to 12 months to do, and anyone that comes now with a sidecar wanting to do it by year end, that&#8217;s wholly unrealistic timelines in my view, there&#8217;s a fair amount of diligence that&#8217;s required.&#8221;</p>
<p>There also needs to be an understanding of the additional complexities that go into structuring and creating a transaction that needs to take into account both the insurance liabilities and asset management sides of the equation.</p>
<p>Trahan continued, &#8220;There&#8217;s a lot of discussion you have with the other side, that is either the securities team of a broker or generally with counterparties, that if they discussed ILS markets in the past, they probably discussed using cash, T-bills as collateral. So starting to discuss CLOs and rated feeder notes, that generally tends to bring a different group within that organisation, or you only end up working with bigger organisations. Or if you&#8217;re dealing with an MGA group, you bring fronters into the mix, and that just makes things more complex and just takes more time. That&#8217;s another dynamic, another element that we find.&#8221;</p>
<p>Trahan also discussed the critical importance of alignment in casualty ILS transactions, highlighting that expectations need to be set, particularly around alignment on both the potential upside and downside of any partnership arrangement on casualty risks.</p>
<p>He explained, &#8220;The last element I would point to is willingness for counterparties to share in the upside and the downside. So having to further strengthen the alignment, sliding scale commissions for example is one of those elements that just show more alignment. Because ultimately they&#8217;ll know their book way better than we will.</p>
<p>&#8220;We&#8217;ll spend a lot of time discussing how you come with that view, and then we&#8217;ll build in a slide around it. This is not me or you trying to see whether I have a different view, a lower view of your loss ratio, so there&#8217;s an asymmetric transaction that takes place. That&#8217;s not how we think of it, so that requires more time as well.&#8221;</p>
<p>Trahan&#8217;s comments all speak to the complexity of casualty risks and the structural innovation that has come about through the use of insurance-linked securities technology to enable investors to participate in these areas of insurance.</p>
<p>They also highlight the need for a disciplined approach and a willingness to fully-engage with partners on transactions, to avoid any unexpected or uncertain outcomes.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.artemis.bm/news/in-casualty-ils-motivations-are-key-timelines-must-be-realistic-trahan-calidris/">In casualty ILS motivations are key, timelines must be realistic: Trahan, Calidris</a> was published by: <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.Artemis.bm</a><br />
Our <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/deal-directory/">catastrophe bond deal directory</a><br />
Sign up for <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/e-newsletter/">our free weekly email newsletter here</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
		<post-id xmlns="com-wordpress:feed-additions:1">150971</post-id>	</item>
		<item>
		<title>ILS investors need to speak up and ask for alternative model views: Karen Clark</title>
		<link>https://www.artemis.bm/news/ils-investors-need-to-speak-up-and-ask-for-alternative-model-views-karen-clark/?utm_source=rss&#038;utm_medium=rss&#038;utm_campaign=ils-investors-need-to-speak-up-and-ask-for-alternative-model-views-karen-clark</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Steve Evans]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 18:15:19 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[Insurance Linked Investments]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Risk Modelling]]></category>
