		<category><![CDATA[Third-party reinsurance capital]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.artemis.bm/?p=150708</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>This content is copyright to <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.artemis.bm</a> and should not appear anywhere else, or an infringement has occurred.</p>
<p>Captives are rapidly evolving from risk retention vehicles into strategic capital access platforms, which in turn is beginning to attract third-party capital, according to broker Aon, who emphasises that captive owners are increasingly partnering with external investors to unlock capacity that supports growth initiatives and long-term resilience. In a recent article, the broker underscores that [&#8230;]</p>
<p><a href="https://www.artemis.bm/news/evolving-captives-becoming-strategic-platforms-for-attracting-third-party-capital-aon/">Evolving captives becoming strategic platforms for attracting third-party capital: Aon</a> was published by: <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.Artemis.bm</a><br />
Our <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/deal-directory/">catastrophe bond deal directory</a><br />
Sign up for <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/e-newsletter/">our free weekly email newsletter here</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>This content is copyright to <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.artemis.bm</a> and should not appear anywhere else, or an infringement has occurred.</p>
<p>Captives are rapidly evolving from risk retention vehicles into strategic capital access platforms, which in turn is beginning to attract third-party capital, according to broker Aon, who emphasises that captive owners are increasingly partnering with external investors to unlock capacity that supports growth initiatives and long-term resilience.<br />
<span id="more-150708"></span><br />
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" data-attachment-id="83452" data-permalink="https://www.artemis.bm/news/local-investors-took-meaningful-chunk-of-greater-bay-re-cat-bond-aon/attachment/aon-logo-3/" data-orig-file="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/aon-logo.png" data-orig-size="884,542" data-comments-opened="0" data-image-title="aon-logo" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-large-file="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/aon-logo.png" class="alignright wp-image-83452" src="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/aon-logo.png" alt="aon-logo" width="370" height="227" srcset="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/aon-logo.png 884w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/aon-logo-300x184.png 300w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/aon-logo-768x471.png 768w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/aon-logo-98x60.png 98w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/aon-logo-200x123.png 200w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/aon-logo-350x215.png 350w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 370px) 100vw, 370px" />In a recent article, the broker underscores that captives today offer much more than an opportunity to reduce insurance costs and retain risk, noting that they are now being widely used to help businesses make strategic decisions around capital deployment and long-term resilience.</p>
<p>Aon also added that the evolving role of captives reflects a key challenge facing business leaders.</p>
<p>“Risks are changing faster than available insurance capacity. Losses from growing natural catastrophe, cyber and litigation exposures add pressure to balance sheets. In response, insurers are deploying capital more selectively, at times reducing line sizes and capacity. The resulting supply-demand imbalance has widened the gap between the risks organizations need to finance and the protection available through traditional markets,” the broker explained.</p>
<p>In addition, the broker highlighted that market leaders utilise captives to bridge this gap and strengthen their broader risk financing strategies.</p>
<p>Besides filling coverage gaps, captives also allow organisations to aggregate enterprise-wide exposures, access new sources of capital and unlock risk transfer solutions, such as insurance-linked securities (ILS).</p>
<p>Within a volatile risk environment, the broking group observed that key market participants are increasingly gravitating toward financing structures that deliver liquidity, expedited recovery capabilities, and enhanced predictability in the aftermath of loss occurrences.</p>
<p>In essence, captives function as a notable conduit to these mechanisms, enabling businesses to integrate parametric, reinsurance, and other alternative risk transfer methodologies into a single risk financing framework.</p>
<p>“The growth of ILS and other structured capital solutions is creating new ways to finance risk. Captives can help organizations access these sources of capital by serving as a bridge between enterprise exposures and investors willing to assume specific risks,” Aon explained.</p>
<p>The broker added: “Some of the most innovative captive strategies are beginning to attract third-party capital. Rather than relying exclusively on corporate capital, captive owners are partnering with external investors to unlock capacity that can support larger retention strategies, growth initiatives and resilience objectives.”</p>
<p>Aon concludes: &#8220;Captives are evolving from risk retention vehicles into capital access platforms, helping organizations connect exposures with the most efficient mix of traditional, alternative and third-party capital.&#8221;</p>
<p><a href="https://www.artemis.bm/news/evolving-captives-becoming-strategic-platforms-for-attracting-third-party-capital-aon/">Evolving captives becoming strategic platforms for attracting third-party capital: Aon</a> was published by: <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.Artemis.bm</a><br />
Our <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/deal-directory/">catastrophe bond deal directory</a><br />
Sign up for <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/e-newsletter/">our free weekly email newsletter here</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
